{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Between the evenings</b> — from six hours and onward is called בֵּין הָעַרְבָּיִם, for the sun inclines toward its place of setting to evening. And the expression בֵּין הָעַרְבָּיִם appears in my eyes to mean those hours that are between the eveninging of the day and the eveninging of the night. The eveninging of the day is at the beginning of the seventh hour, for \"the shadows of evening lengthen,\" and the eveninging of the night is at the beginning of the night. עֶרֶב — an expression of dusk and darkness, as in \"all joy has reached evening\" (Isaiah 24:11), in the language of Rashi. (Rashi on Exodus 12:6) And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] objected from the verse that says (Exodus 30:8) \"and when Aaron kindles the lamps between the evenings,\" for there it is written (Exodus 27:21) \"Aaron and his sons shall arrange it from evening to morning,\" and furthermore from what is written concerning the pesach itself: \"there you shall slaughter the pesach in the evening, at the going down of the sun, the appointed time of your departure from Egypt\" (Deuteronomy 16:6), for \"the going down of the sun\" means the setting of the sun. But this is no refutation of the Rav, for our Sages already said (Berakhot 9a): \"In the evening you slaughter, at the going down of the sun you eat, the appointed time of your departure from Egypt you burn.\" And the Rav already wrote this:<br>And what seems correct to me in this matter is that the night is called עֶרֶב, as it says (Exodus 12:18) \"in the evening you shall eat unleavened bread,\" and that is at night, as it says (Exodus 12:8) \"and they shall eat the flesh on this night, roasted with fire, and unleavened bread,\" and likewise \"and it was evening and it was morning\" (Genesis 1:5) — referring to the beginning of the night, at the emergence of the stars, and likewise \"in the twilight, in the evening of the day, in the blackness of night and darkness\" (Proverbs 7:9). And the end of the day is also called עֶרֶב, as they said: \"the two angels came to Sodom in the evening, and Lot was sitting at the gate of Sodom\" (Genesis 19:1). And likewise \"it was in the evening, and the quail came up\" (Exodus 16:13) — and the quail did not come at night. And likewise many others. And it is written \"evening and morning and noon I meditate and moan\" (Psalms 55:18):<br>Now these three times encompass the entire day. If so, after noon it is immediately called עֶרֶב, and at sunrise and all the time the sun is in the east it is called בֹּקֶר, and that is four hours, as they testified concerning the morning tamid that it is offered at four hours (Eduyot 6:1), and after the morning the time is called צָהֳרַיִם, as it says \"from morning until noon\" (I Kings 18:26), and these are the fifth and sixth hours, and it is from the expression \"a צֹהַר you shall make for the ark\" (Genesis 6:16), like the meaning of זֹהַר (brightness). And it uses the plural form for them because they are two. Now they are two noons, either because the light shines in all directions — for in the morning the light is in the east, and in the evening it is in the west, and at midday in the height of the firmament it illuminates all directions — and when the noon hours pass and the sun departs from shining on both sides, it is called עַרְבַּיִם because the sun \"evens\" from both those sides, and this time is all the while the sun shines in the firmament. But at the setting of the sun, which is about an hour and a quarter according to our Sages (Pesahim 58b), it is not the time of slaughter and is not called עַרְבַּיִם, but rather it is עֶרֶב יוֹם (evening of the day). And it says <b>between the evenings</b>, for <b>between</b> in this place does not serve to separate, but rather it is like the sense of \"within them,\" and it is like \"let there be a treaty between us, between us and between you\" (Genesis 26:28), \"what is it between me and between you\" (Genesis 23:15), \"among thick branches\" (Ezekiel 19:11), \"in the midst of the wheel\" (Ezekiel 10:6) — like \"within them,\" \"from among the burning\" (Numbers 17:2) — \"from within it,\" as in the sense of \"and she rose while it was still night\" (Proverbs 31:15) — \"in the midst of the night,\" and likewise <b>between the evenings</b>, and it does not say בָּעַרְבָּיִם, so that it not be understood as the evening of many days. Now Scripture said that the pesach is slaughtered in the midst of the evenings, for the time of slaughter according to the Torah is from six hours and onward until the beginning of the setting of the sun, and likewise it says (Leviticus 23:5) \"on the fourteenth of the month, between the evenings, is YHWH's pesach\" — that is the time of slaughter, and likewise (Numbers 9:3) \"between the evenings you shall make it\" — concerning the beginning of the making, which is the slaughter, and likewise (Exodus 16:12) \"between the evenings you shall eat flesh\" — those are the aforementioned hours, for they had a long time for eating the flesh. And it says (Exodus 16:13) \"it was in the evening and the quail came up,\" for it came on the first day at one hour into the evening:<br>And it is possible that the expression follows Rashi's explanation — two evenings: evening of morning and evening of day — for thus Scripture says concerning the morning meal-offering and the evening meal-offering, as it says (II Kings 3:20) \"it was in the morning, when the meal-offering went up,\" and it says (Ezra 9:4–5) \"until the evening meal-offering... and at the evening meal-offering I arose from my affliction.\" And מִנְחָה is an expression of the resting of the sun and the quieting of its great light, as we translate (Genesis 3:8) \"לְמִנְחַ יוֹמָא\" (at the leisure of the day), and these are the great מִנְחָה and the small מִנְחָה that the Sages mentioned (Berakhot 26b). Now all this time is for the kindling of the lamps and for the incense, which are not valid at night except during the time of the tamid of between the evenings and preceding it. And to this Onkelos is inclined (Onkelos on Exodus 12:6), who said \"between the suns\" — the sun of the east and the sun of the west, and Scripture says (Exodus 27:21) \"Aaron shall arrange it from evening to morning,\" that they should burn all night:<br>Now we can explain \"there you shall slaughter the pesach in the evening\" (Deuteronomy 16:6) as referring to the aforementioned time, for it is called עֶרֶב, and \"at the going down of the sun, the appointed time of your departure from Egypt\" would be joined with \"and you shall boil and eat,\" and likewise many others. But in my opinion, \"you shall slaughter the pesach\" does not refer to slaughter, but rather says: in the place that HaShem your Elohim will choose, there you shall make the sacrifice of the pesach at night, at the going down of the sun, which is the appointed time of your departure from Egypt, as it says (Deuteronomy 16:1) \"HaShem your Elohim brought you out from Egypt at night.\" And concerning this act it says \"and you shall boil and eat\" in that aforementioned place, and the pesach itself is called זֶבַח, as in \"you shall not slaughter upon leavened bread the blood of my sacrifice\" (Exodus 34:25) — my pesach. And this is the meaning of the entire passage (Deuteronomy 16:1): \"Observe the month of Aviv and make a pesach to HaShem your Elohim, for in the month of Aviv HaShem your Elohim brought you out from Egypt at night,\" and so forth, \"you shall not eat leavened bread with it\" — it mentions the act and the night, and commands how they shall eat it, and it mentions the seven days (verses 3–4), and in all that passage there is no mention of the fourteenth day and the slaughter on it, and likewise it says (Deuteronomy 16:4) \"and no flesh of that which you slaughter in the evening on the first day shall remain until morning.\" Now \"in the evening on the first day\" — the fifteenth day, the first of the seven aforementioned days — for the fourteenth day is not mentioned here at all, and likewise (Exodus 12:18) \"on the fourteenth day of the month, in the evening, you shall eat unleavened bread\" — that is the night of the fifteenth. Now the meaning of \"which you slaughter in the evening\" is: it shall not remain until morning, of the flesh that you make into a sacrifice in the evening of the first day:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Maimonides (\"the Rav\") with introductory formula (\"the Rav already wrote this\"), after which a long passage of linguistic argumentation follows presenting the Rav's view; the transition back to Ramban's own voice occurs at \"But in my opinion.\""}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:07:37.182021025Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}